"id": "learned-syntax",
"el": "Λόγια σύνταξη",
"title": "Learned syntax: the formal layer at C1",
"span": "B2–C1",
"blurb": "The older constructions that survive in formal Greek: learned prepositions with other cases (υπό, επί, περί, εκ/εξ, άνευ, μετά + genitive, προ, διά, εν + dative), the many uses of ως (ως εξής, ως γνωστόν, ως επί το πλείστον), formal conjunctions (εφόσον, δεδομένου ότι, μολονότι, ούτως ώστε, καθότι), and the frozen verb forms of official texts (έστω, δέον, ουδείς, ως έχει).",
"intro": [
{
"p": "Chapter 40 covered the formal forms you need for a B2 letter. At C1 the reading texts (law, press, essays, speeches) use a further layer of learned syntax. Much of it lives in **fixed phrases**: you do not build new ones, you recognise and reuse them. Each section gives the phrases with their everyday equivalents, so you can move between registers in both directions."
},
